      "text": "NOTES",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "NOTES",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "nibuHqaZYE812gwac8yVq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 127.34578074414065,
      "y": 1677.3759769999997,
      "width": 906.7975463867188,
      "height": 500.78125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1K",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1718277833,
      "version": 1712,
      "versionNonce": 269148935,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1794910297669,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Scope = which threads can see the variable.\nLifetime = how long it keeps its value.\n\nThread scope: every thread gets its own private copy. One million threads, one million copies.\n\nBlock scope: every block gets its own copy, and the threads inside share it.\n\nLocal arrays go to local memory (slow), unless every index is a constant.\n\nShared memory is where a block keeps the piece of global data it reuses.\n\nConstant memory: kernels can only read it. Cached, so reads are fast. \n\nGlobal variables carry data from one kernel launch to the next, but are bad style:\nuse sparingly.\n\nThe keywords are CUDA C. The cuda-oxide way to write them is not verified yet.\n\nRule of thumb: the fewer threads that can see a variable, the faster its memory.\nLocal arrays are the exception: private, but slow. ",
      "fontSize": 20.03125,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Scope = which threads can see the variable.\nLifetime = how long it keeps its value.\n\nThread scope: every thread gets its own private copy. One million threads, one million copies.\n\nBlock scope: every block gets its own copy, and the threads inside share it.\n\nLocal arrays go to local memory (slow), unless every index is a constant.\n\nShared memory is where a block keeps the piece of global data it reuses.\n\nConstant memory: kernels can only read it. Cached, so reads are fast. \n\nGlobal variables carry data from one kernel launch to the next, but are bad style:\nuse sparingly.\n\nThe keywords are CUDA C. The cuda-oxide way to write them is not verified yet.\n\nRule of thumb: the fewer threads that can see a variable, the faster its memory.\nLocal arrays are the exception: private, but slow. ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "6xrAAeRzJwDMTMP7xkbRK",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1110.3681174623482,
      "y": 932.4638671413294,
      "width": 1096.29931640625,
      "height": 600,
      "angle": 0,
