      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why registers are faster than shared and global memory:\n\n1. No load instruction. An arithmetic instruction names its registers directly, so the core \ncan use the values straight away. A value in shared or global memory first needs a separate load\ninstruction to copy it into a register.\n\n2. They sit right next to the core. Registers are on the chip, inside the processor itself. Shared \nmemory is also on-chip but a step further away, so its load has a short wait. Global memory is off-chip, \nso its load waits hundreds of cycles.\n\n3. No bandwidth used. Reading a register takes nothing from the limited global memory bandwidth, so more\nof that bandwidth is left for the data that really must come from global memory.\n\n4. Less energy. Reading a register costs at least 10x less energy than reading global memory.\n\nRule of thumb:\nRegister = 1 instruction, no wait.\nShared memory = 2 instructions, short wait.\nGlobal memory = 2 instructions, long wait.\n\nThe only caveat with using registers is that the number of registers available to each thread is quite limited in\ntoday's GPUs. As we saw in Chapter 4, the occupancy achieved for an application can be reduced if the register\nusage exceeds the limit for full-occupancy scenarios. Thus, we need also to avoid oversubscribing to the limited\nresource whenever possible.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why registers are faster than shared and global memory:\n\n1. No load instruction. An arithmetic instruction names its registers directly, so the core \ncan use the values straight away. A value in shared or global memory first needs a separate load\ninstruction to copy it into a register.\n\n2. They sit right next to the core. Registers are on the chip, inside the processor itself. Shared \nmemory is also on-chip but a step further away, so its load has a short wait. Global memory is off-chip, \nso its load waits hundreds of cycles.\n\n3. No bandwidth used. Reading a register takes nothing from the limited global memory bandwidth, so more\nof that bandwidth is left for the data that really must come from global memory.\n\n4. Less energy. Reading a register costs at least 10x less energy than reading global memory.\n\nRule of thumb:\nRegister = 1 instruction, no wait.\nShared memory = 2 instructions, short wait.\nGlobal memory = 2 instructions, long wait.\n\nThe only caveat with using registers is that the number of registers available to each thread is quite limited in\ntoday's GPUs. As we saw in Chapter 4, the occupancy achieved for an application can be reduced if the register\nusage exceeds the limit for full-occupancy scenarios. Thus, we need also to avoid oversubscribing to the limited\nresource whenever possible.",
